  "close_rendering": "In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.",
  "whats_happening": "The Hebrew Bible opens with seven words that name a beginning, an act, an actor, and two objects, and says nothing about method, material, or duration. The sentence is built so simply that almost every question a modern reader brings to it is left unanswered on purpose. The one genuine grammatical question is whether the first word starts a complete sentence or introduces a clause that waits for its main verb.",
  "key_words": [
    {
      "surface": "בְּרֵאשִׁ֖ית",
      "lemma": "רֵאשִׁית",
      "gloss": "beginning",
      "note": "A noun meaning first or chief part, prefixed with the preposition bə-. Whether it stands alone or is bound to what follows is the central question of the verse.",
      "strongs": "H7225"
    },
    {
      "surface": "בָּרָ֣א",
      "lemma": "בָּרָא",
      "gloss": "he created",
      "note": "A Qal perfect. The verb is used in the Hebrew Bible with God as its subject and never takes a stated material.",
      "strongs": "H1254a"
    },
    {
      "surface": "אֱלֹהִ֑ים",
      "lemma": "אֱלֹהִים",
      "gloss": "God",
      "note": "Plural in form. Here it governs a singular verb, which is the ordinary pattern when the word refers to the God of Israel.",
      "strongs": "H0430"
    },
    {
      "surface": "אֵ֥ת",
      "lemma": "אֵת",
      "gloss": "[direct object]",
      "note": "An untranslatable particle marking a definite direct object. Its presence twice shows both nouns are objects of the one verb.",
      "strongs": "H0853"
    }
  ],
  "translation_note": "English needs a tense the Hebrew does not have. The perfect בָּרָא marks completed action, not a past tense, so In the beginning God created and When God began to create are both defensible renderings of the same consonants.",
